static.solution:
z is a function of two variables x and y which are functions of two variables u and v, the two chain rule identities which apply are: \frac{\partial z}{\partial u} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial u}+ \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial u} and \frac{\partial z}{\partial v} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial v}+ \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial v}
This problem is most easily solved by substitution: z = \sin\mathopen{}\left(\left(u\cos\mathopen{}\left(v\right)\right)^{2}+\left(u\sin\mathopen{}\left(v\right)\right)^{2}\right) = \sin\mathopen{}\left(u^{2}\right) so \frac{\partial z}{\partial u} = \cos\mathopen{}\left(u^{2}\right)\cdot 2u, and \frac{\partial z}{\partial v} = 0.
This problem can also be solved using the chain rule but it is more difficult.
